gh a fork of GPTQ code) and RTN as a simpler baseline. Actually, the QuaRot repo has both RTN and GPTQ options via `--wbits`, `--true-sequential`, etc. Let me recall: QuaRot repo `qua_rot.py` has GPTQ-like quantization; there's `--rtn` flag maybe? Actually in QuaRot, weight quantization is done via GPTQ-style; rotation via Hadamard. The QuaRot README lists commands for quantizing LLAMA-2 7B/13B/70B. It uses GPTQ for weights. There is also \"naive\" round-to-nearest baseline. Hmm.\n\nActually the QuaRot repo (spcl/QuaRot) contains:\n- `qua_rot.py` - quantization script with argparse including `--model`, `--wbits` (4, 8, 16), `--groupsize`, `--nsamples`, `--rotate`, `--percdamp`, `--seed`, etc.\n- `datautils.py` - dataset loading (c4 and ptb and wikitext2) for calibration.\n- The quantization is GPTQ-based (from GPTQ repo) with rotation.\n- There's also `--nearest` flag? Hmm, not sure. Actually QuaRot has both GPTQ and RTN modes? The `--nearest` flag in the GPTQ code chooses round-to-nearest instead of GPTQ's greedy.\n\nFor RTN: in the original GPTQ repo, `--nearest` selects RTN. QuaRot likely inherits that.\n\n- Supported architectures: QuaRot originally supports LLaMA (LlamaForCausalLM). Since Phi-3 is not Llama architecture, it likely requires adaptation. Phi-3 uses `Phi3ForCausalLM` with its own rotary embedding implementation (though it's similar). The official QuaRot may not support it out of the box, requiring adding Phi-3 to the list of supported model classes and handling the rotary module.\n\n- The paper evaluation: perplexity on WikiText-2 (and maybe C4), zero-shot on OpenLLM / lm-eval-harness tasks (e.g., ARC-e, ARC-c, HellaSwag, Winogrande, PIQA, etc.), and LMEval. The QuaRot paper reports on LM-Eval tasks.\n\n- 6-bit: GPTQ supports arbitrary wbits (4, 8, etc.), so 6-bit is feasible in principle; QuaRot's README typically uses 4-bit/8-bit. So 6-bit needs `--wbits 6`.\n\nPhi-3-mini-4k-instruct: 3.8B params, Llama-2-like but with its own architecture (Phi3ForCausalLM), 32 layers, hidden 3072, 32 heads, uses rotary embeddings (RoPE), QK-norm, etc. Vocabulary 32064.\n\nNow the design task. The user wants:\n- Compare QuaRot-RTN vs QuaRot-GPTQ on Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct at 4, 6, 8 bits.\n- Metrics: perplexity and zero-shot accuracy.\n- Controls/baselines: FP16 reference, non-QuaRot RTN/GPTQ baseline.\n\nI should structure this into a plan:\n\n**Fixed requirements (from project brief):**\n- Model: Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct\n- Precisions: INT4, INT6, INT8 (i.e., wbits 4, 6, 8)\n- Methods: QuaRot-RTN, QuaRot-GPTQ (i.e., with and without rotation in each)\n- Metrics: perplexity, zero-shot accuracy\n\n**Proposed choices:**\n- Perplexity corpus: WikiText-2 test set (standard), possibly also C4 as secondary — QuaRot paper uses both. Use sliding window (stride model.config.max_position_embeddings - 256? standard usage in GPTQ repo uses seqlen 2048, stride=2048 on wikitext2).\n- Zero-shot tasks: lm-eval-harness suite, a subset of OpenLLM tasks: ARC-c, ARC-e, HellaSwag, PIQA, Winogrande, BoolQ, maybe OpenBookQA. Report accuracy / acc_norm.\n- Quantization config: groupsize 128, percdamp 0.01, nsamples 128 (GPTQ calibration), seqlen 2048, calibration data: c4 (common) or wikitext2 — QuaRot default uses c4 with 128 samples (or 1024?). Need to pick. Actually QuaRot GPTQ default nsamples... The GPTQ repo default is 128 samples of c4 at seqlen 2048. QuaRot uses same. Let me note both as options.\n- Seeds: 3 seeds for quantization (to handle GPTQ randomness), report mean±std.\n- Baselines: FP16 (unquantized reference), and \"vanilla RTN\" (no rotation) and \"vanilla GPTQ\" (no rotation) at each bits — actually the fixed comparison is QuaRot-RTN vs QuaRot-GPTQ. Adding non-QuaRot baselines isolates the effect of rotation. Might be good but could be optional. Since RTN determinism.\n\nWait — need to clarify: \"QuaRot-RTN\" and \"QuaRot-GPTQ\": QuaRot applies rotation to both weights and activations, then quantizes weights (activations optional). RTN vs GPTQ refers to the weight quantization method within QuaRot. So the design should hold rotation fixed (QuaRot rotation) and vary weight quantizer (RTN vs GPTQ). To isolate, add no-rotation control.\n\n- Analysis plan: \n  - PPL per condition (method × bits × seed), compare each against FP16; report ΔPPL and relative PPL increase.\n  - Zero-shot: mean accuracy across tasks per condition, per-task table, compute average over tasks (macro-average); also possibly aggregate via lm-eval's default. Report mean±SD across seeds.\n  - Statistical comparison: paired comparison per seed between RTN and GPTQ; report effect sizes at each bit; assess monotonicity in bits.\n  - Note quantization speed/latency maybe optional but not required (design focuses on quality metrics).\n\n- Controls: \n  - FP16 reference (no quantization).\n  - Same calibration/training config between RTN and GPTQ.\n  - Fixed random seed for data shuffling/calibration selection.\n  - Same lm-eval harness version and task config for all conditions.\n  - Hardware: run all conditions on same GPU; use eager attention for quantization.\n\n- Feasibility: Phi-3-mini ~3.8B params; INT4 quantized ~2.4GB; full precision ~7.6GB (fp16) + calibration activations. On a 24GB GPU feasible. CPU 224 cores — quantization GPTQ is compute heavy but feasible; zero-shot eval on CPU might be slow but doable with large tasks like HellaSwag. Note network disabled → model/dataset download not possible offline; require pre-cached HF model & datasets. Flag this.\n\nWait — network disabled! So downloading Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct, WikiText-2, C4, lm-eval tasks is not possible in this environment. The environment has no GPU and no network and no datasets. This is a design-only task, so feasibility must be framed as a designed procedure with stated environment requirements (i.e., this environment is not able to run it; the design is for a suitably provisioned environment). I must flag that. The task.json explicitly says \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" So the design is purely a written plan.\n\n**Assumptions/uncertainties:**\n1. The official QuaRot repo (spcl/QuaRot) does not natively support Phi-3 (Phi3ForCausalLM) — need to verify and possibly adapt (add Phi3 model class to supported list, ensure rotary modules handled). This is the biggest risk. I cannot verify from the repo since it's absent.\n2. 6-bit support: GPTQ supports arbitrary bits; QuaRot README documents 4/8. RTN at 6-bit fine. Need to confirm the code path handles wbits=6 (it should, since integer arithmetic on wbits).\n3. WikiText-2 as PPL corpus and calibration dataset: QuaRot default calibration c4; paper PPL on c4 and wikitext2. Need alignment to avoid calibration/eval leakage (use wikitext2 for eval, c4 for calibration, or split wikitext2 train for calibration and test for eval).\n4. lm-eval-harness version pinning: different versions give different numbers; pick a specific version (e.g., 0.4.x) and report only relative comparisons.\n5. Rotary embedding handling for Phi-3 — QuaRot's Hadamard rotation is applied on weight & activation subspaces; phi-3 rotary uses different interleaving? Phi-3 uses `Phi3RotaryEmbedding` similar to Llama but with different head_dim handling? Might need adaptation so the rotation is applied consistently.\n6. Model is instruct variant: PPL on instruct model may behave differently; that's fine as long as consistent.\n7. Memory/compute feasibility for the designed procedure (assume ≥24GB GPU; GPTQ quantization on CPU is slow — but design could recommend GPU).\n8. Determinism/seed control: RTN is deterministic; GPTQ is order-dependent and seed-dependent → multiple seeds.\n9. Whether activation quantization (QuaRot also quantizes activations) is in scope. The question asks about RTN vs GPTQ weight quantization. I'll treat activation quantization as fixed at 8-bit or included as part of QuaRot's default (4-bit weights + 8-bit activations). To keep it clean: apply QuaRot rotation + weight quantization at 4/6/8-bit, activations at 8-bit (QuaRot default) — but note this as a choice to hold fixed across conditions.\n\nActually, QuaRot's runtime (quarot_utils) quantizes activations to 8-bit by default in the deploy/inference path. For evaluating quality, the standard approach in the QuaRot paper: they evaluate both weight-only (W4A16) and fully quantized (W4A8). To compare RTN vs GPTQ cleanly, hold activation precision fixed (e.g., A16 for weight-only evaluation, or A8 for full). I'll propose primary: weight-only (activations unquantized) to isolate weight-quantization method, with a secondary W4A8 check. Hmm but the question is about \"RTN and GPTQ compare in perplexity and zero-shot accuracy\" — the difference is the weight quantizer. Keeping activations FP16 isolates that. But QuaRot's whole point is rotating both and quantizing both. I'll propose: primary condition = rotation + weight quant at B-bit with activations full precision (to isolate weight quantizer effect), and note a secondary condition with A8 activations. Actually to keep design clean and answer the question directly: the two methods both include QuaRot rotation; they differ only in weight quantizer. So fix activations as full precision (W only) to isolate. Mention A8 as robustness check. That's a reasonable \"proposed choice.\"\n\nLet me structure the plan handoff now. Keep it structured and reasonably concise but complete. I'll do a couple more quick environment checks? The key facts: workspace empty (only task.json), no network, no GPU, no packages, no datasets. That's enough for the inspection report. Maybe verify no datasets present anywhere.\n\nLet me do one more check to be thorough about whether there is any hidden content (e.g., no datasets).", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "echo \"HOME=$HOME\"; ls -la ~ 2>/dev/null; ls -la ~/.cache 2>/dev/null; find / -maxdepth 3 -type d \\( -name \"datasets\" -o -name \"hug