e. Keep it structured and complete but not the full final design.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "VERIFIED REPOSITORY STATE (honest finding):\n- /workspace contains exactly one file: task.json (plus track.md referenced inside it). There is NO QuaRot codebase, README, configs, or scripts present.\n- The referenced work neurips2024/94328 does not exist anywhere in the sandbox (searched / for 94328/*qua* dirs, .git dirs, python files outside system libs).\n- Environment: Python 3.14.4 only; no torch/transformers/lm-eval-harness/GPTQ packages installed; no datasets or HF cache; no GPU (cpu_affinity_count 224); network unreachable.\nConsequence: I cannot verify from disk any of the requested specifics (QuaRot README, RTN/GPTQ script flags, supported architectures, calibration-dataset defaults, PPL/zero-shot eval setup). Everything below about the real QuaRot repo is background knowledge, explicitly flagged as to-be-verified, not observed findings.\n\nPROPOSED APPROACH (outline only — full design deferred pending approval):\n\n1) Experimental conditions (the 6 target cells required by the brief):\n   - Methods (fixed): QuaRot-RTN, QuaRot-GPTQ — both with QuaRot Hadamard rotation applied to weights (and same rotation config); they differ ONLY in the weight quantizer (round-to-nearest vs GPTQ greedy). Activations held at full precision in the primary eval so the weight-quantizer comparison is isolated.\n   - Precisions (fixed, from brief): 4-bit, 6-bit, 8-bit weight quantization (wbits=4/6/8), groupsize=128.\n   - So: 2 methods × 3 precisions = 6 quantized conditions.\n\n2) Controls / baselines:\n   - FP16 unquantized reference model (upper-bound, always evaluated under identical protocol).\n   - Rotation-isolation controls (proposed): non-QuaRot \"plain RTN\" and \"plain GPTQ\" (no Hadamard rotation) at 4-bit and 8-bit, so the added value of rotation can be separated from the quantizer choice.\n   - Activation-quantization robustness check (proposed, optional secondary): repeat the 6 target cells with QuaRot's 8-bit activation quantization (W4A8/W6A8/W8A8) to confirm conclusions are not artifacts of weight-only eval.\n   - Protocol controls: identical calibration docs/order, identical seed handling, identical lm-eval harness version/tasks, identical sequence lengths, same GPU and torch settings across all conditions.\n\n3) Calibration protocol (proposed): GPTQ path calibrates on a fixed 128-sample, 2048-token subset (C4 'en' — the QuaRot/GPTQ default — or WikiText-2 train-split if the repo defaults there; must match the repo's datautils loader). Seed the calibration-document selection and set percdamp=0.01 as in GPTQ defaults. RTN needs no calibration data but is fed the identical random seed for parity. Run 3 quantization seeds per target cell to bound GPTQ's order-dependent variance.\n\n4) Evaluation protocol (proposed):\n   - Perplexity: WikiText-2 test set with sliding-window stride (standard HuggingFace/GPTQ evaluation script), seqlen 2048, reporting token-normalized PPL.\n   - Zero-shot: lm-eval-harness (pinned version, e.g., 0.4.x) on a standard small-LLM task subset — ARC-easy, ARC-challenge, HellaSwag, PIQA, Winogrande, BoolQ — reporting the standard accuracy/acc_norm per task and a macro-average across tasks. Instruct variant does not use chat template (plain LM continuation).\n\n5) Analysis / aggregation plan (proposed): per-cell PPL mean±SD over seeds; ΔPPL and relative PPL increase vs FP16; per-task zero-shot accuracy plus macro-average per cell; paired comparison of QuaRot-RTN vs QuaRot-GPTQ at each precision (same seeds); monotonicity check across 4/6/8-bit; sanity check that rotation improves 4-bit PPL/acc over plain RTN/GPTQ. Report numbers as designed outputs only; no run is executed here.\n\n6) Feasibility framing: this sandbox cannot run anything (no network, no GPU, no packages, no repo). The design is written for a provisioned environment with internet (or pre-cached model/datasets), ≥1 GPU with ≥24GB VRAM (Phi-3mini ~3.8B params; fp16 ~7.6GB weights + calibration activations), and the official spcl/QuaRot repo installed with its dependency set. CPU-only fallback is possible but slow; noted as a risk.\n\nFIXED (from brief) vs PROPOSED:\n- Fixed: model=Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct; precisions INT4/INT6/INT8; methods QuaRot-RTN and QuaRot-GPTQ; metrics perplexity and zero-shot accuracy.\n- Proposed (mine): PPL corpus (WikiText-2 test), zero-shot task subset + harness version, calibration data/size/seed protocol, groupsize=128, percdamp=0.01, FP16 reference + plain-RTN/plain-GPTQ controls, activation-precision handling, seed replication (3 seeds), aggregation (macro-average + paired comparison). These are all adjustable if the researcher has preferences.", "evidence": "Filesystem inspection: `ls -la /workspace` shows only task.json (no QuaRot repo/README/configs/scripts). `find / -name .git -type d` and searches for 94328/qua* returned nothing. `pip list` shows no torch/transformers/GPTQ/lm-eval-harness/datasets; Python is 3.14.4. No datasets or HF cache under /home/actor or /. Network check (`/dev/tcp/8.8.8.8/53`) failed with \"Network is unreachable\". No GPU (gpu_enabled=false; 224 CPU cores). task.json track.md: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"", "assumptions": ["The official QuaRot implementation (spcl/QuaRot) does NOT natively support Phi-3 (Phi3ForCausalLM / Phi-3 rotary embedding layout) and will likely need a small adaptation (register the Phi3 model class, ensure Hadamard rotation is applied to the correct rotary/QKV weight subspaces). Cannot be verified because the repo is absent from this sandbox. This is the single biggest technical risk.", "The GPTQ path in QuaRot supports arbitrary wbits (4/6/8); the README documents 4 and 8 but wbits is a general int. RTN supports any bits trivially. 6-bit must be confirmed against the repo's quantize_weights code (no 4/8-only guards)."