 4/6/8-bit, and a sanity check that rotation improves over plain quantizers.\\n\\n6) Feasibility — explicit runtime requirements for a provisioned environment (≥24GB GPU, Python ≤3.11, internet or pre-cached HF assets, official spcl/QuaRot dependency set), plus the honest statement that nothing was executed in this sandbox.\\n\\nThroughout, flag repo-specific details (Phi-3 model registration / rotary-embedding subspace handling for Hadamard rotation, whether wbits=6 is a clean code path, calibration defaults, pinned harness version) as assumptions to verify against the official repository rather than observed facts. Deliver the final design document as the submission candidate.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "6ef566a3-5f70-4daa-8bde-cbc630476f7f", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T15:02:52.544323", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "1defe040-79c7-422b-807a-cc270a8bb1aa", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "The approach is approved and all required components are confirmed from my inspection. I'll now produce the complete final experiment design as the submission candidate.\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher approved the plan and now wants the full final design document as the result-stage handoff. I should produce a comprehensive, well-structured experiment design document incorporating all six requested points, with assumptions flagged, and the honest statement that nothing was executed.\n\nI don't need to run more tools — I have enough evidence. But maybe I could do a quick extra check to be thorough? Not necessary; I already verified the repo state. Let me write the full design.\n\nLet me structure the final design document:\n\n# Experiment Design: QuaRot Generalization to Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct (RTN vs GPTQ at INT4/6/8)\n\n## 0. Document status / honesty\n- Design-only, nothing executed in sandbox.\n\n## 1. Research question & rationale\n- Frame: QuaRot removes activation/weight outliers via Hadamard rotation then quantizes weights (GPTQ-style or RTN). Validated on LLaMA. Question: does the rotation+quantizer decomposition generalize to Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct (non-LLaMA architecture, different rotary embedding / hidden dim / head dim)? Compare RTN vs GPTQ across bits.\n\n## 2. Verified environment state (what was actually observed)\n- only task.json; no QuaRot repo; no packages; no GPU; no network; no datasets.\n\n## 3. Fixed requirements vs proposed choices (table)\n\n## 4. Experimental conditions & controls\nTable of conditions:\n- FP16 reference (control)\n- 6 target cells: QuaRot-RTN and QuaRot-GPTQ × INT4/INT6/INT8\n- Rotation-isolation controls: plain-RTN, plain-GPTQ at INT4 and INT8 (no rotation) — 4 cells\n- Optional secondary: W8A8 activation quantization on the 6 cells + FP16 reference.\n\nState clearly primary comparison: weight-only (activations FP16), groupsize 128.\n\n## 5. Calibration protocol\n- Calibration corpus: match target repo's datautils loader (C4 'en' default, fallback WikiText-2 train). Fixed 128 samples × 2048 tokens. Seeded document selection (seed for shuffling). percdamp=0.01, groupsize=128, true-sequential default as in repo. RTN gets same seed parity (no data needed but same RNG init).\n- Explicit: calibration split ≠ PPL eval split (WikiText-2 test).\n- GPTQ: 3-5 seeds; RTN deterministic single seed but still replicate for parity.\n\n## 6. Evaluation protocol\n- PPL on WikiText-2 test, sliding window stride (e.g., seqlen 2048, stride = seqlen - 256 or full doc approach); standard GPTQ/HF eval script.\n- Zero-shot: lm-eval-harness pinned version (e.g., 0.4.x), 6 tasks: ARC-e, ARC-c, HellaSwag, PIQA, Winogrande, BoolQ; report acc (and acc_norm for HellaSwag/Winogrande/ARC where defined); macro-average.\n- Instruct model: no chat template; plain LM continuation; tokenization with HF AutoTokenizer.\n\n## 7. Analysis & aggregation plan\n- Per-cell mean±SD over seeds.\n- ΔPPL = PPL_cell − PPL_FP16; relative = (PPL_cell/PPL_FP16 − 1).\n- Paired RTN vs GPTQ at each precision under matched seeds.\n- Monotonicity across 4/6/8.\n- Sanity: rotation improves over plain at 4-bit (expect large for RTN, moderate for GPTQ).\n- Thresholds/decision criteria: e.g., rotation benefit = significant Δ; report effect sizes; no formal NHST required but optionally paired t-test/Wilcoxon over seeds — small n caveat.\n- Present per-task table + macro-average.\n\n## 8. Repo-specific verification checklist / assumptions (to verify against official repo once available)\n- Phi-3 model registration (Phi3ForCausalLM), rotary-embedding subspace handling for Hadamard rotation\n- wbits=6 clean path\n- calibration defaults (C4 vs wikitext2, nsamples)\n- pinned lm-eval version\n- script flags (--rotate, --wbits, --groupsize, --percdamp, --seed, --nsamples...)\n\n## 9. Feasibility & runtime requirements\n- ≥24GB GPU (fp16 3.8B ~7.6GB + activations); Python ≤3.11; PyTorch/torch; internet or pre-cached HF assets (model, wikitext, c4 subset, harness); install spcl/QuaRot deps (torch, transformers, datasets, lm_eval, tqdm, etc.).\n- Rough compute estimate: 6 target cells × ~3 seeds + controls; GPTQ calibration minutes on GPU per cell; W8A8 inference; lm-eval 6 tasks per model; total well within a day on one GPU. Acknowledge uncertainty.\n\n## 10. Risk register / mitigations\n- Phi-3 adaptation risk\n- 6-bit support\n- environment constraints\n\n## 11. Deliverables / reporting table format\n- Master results table template.\n\nI'll write this into the answer field for stage=result. Evidence should recap the verified observations. Assumptions list. Uncertainties short.\n\nLet me draft it well and completely, but not overly verbose. This is the submission candidate, so it should be a polished final design document.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "=========================================================================\nEXPERIMENT DESIGN (FINAL, DESIGN-ONLY)\nTitle: Evaluating QuaRot Generalization to Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct:\nComparing QuaRot-RTN vs QuaRot-GPTQ at INT4/INT6/INT8\n=========================================================================\n\n0. DOCUMENT STATUS (honesty clause)\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThis is a DESIGN-ONLY submission. No experiment was executed in the\nsandbox, no results are reported, and no claim of completed runs is made.\nThe sandbox contains only task.json (track.md: \"Design-only task;\nrepositories and compute are not required\"); verified repository findings\nand environment facts are stated in Section 2, and every repo-specific\ndetail is explicitly flagged as an ASSUMPTION TO VERIFY, never as an\nobserved fact.\n\n=========================================================================\n1. RESEARCH QUESTION AND RATIONALE\n=========================================================================\nQuestion (fixed by brief):\n\"HOW WELL DOES QUAROT GENERALIZE TO NON-LLAMA MODELS LIKE\nPhi-3-mini-4k-instruct? How do RTN and GPTQ compare in perplexity and\nzero-shot accuracy across 4-bit, 6-bit, and 8-bit quantization?\"\n\nRationale: QuaRot (NeurIPS 2024) removes activation/weight outliers by\napplying random Hadamard rotations to the input/weight subspaces before\nquantization, then produces quantized weights. Its published validation\nis on LLaMA-class models, whose architecture (rotary embedding layout,\nQKV head structure, hidden sizes) is a known precondition for the\nrotation placement. Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct (3.8B, Phi3ForCausalLM) is a\nnon-LLaMA model: it shares a similar overall decoder design but has its\nown rotary-embedding implementation, QK-norm, different head/hidden\ndims (hidden 3072, 32 heads, 64 head_dim) and vocabulary (32064). The\ndesign therefore tests two things in one experiment:\n  (a) GENERALIZATION: does QuaRot's rotation+quantizer pipeline transfer\n      to a model it was not validated on, measured against an FP16\n      reference and against NON-rotated (plain) quantizers?\n  (b) QUANTIZER COMPARISON: within the QuaRot pipeline, is the weight\n      quantizer (RTN vs GPTQ) still the main accuracy driver, and does\n      the gap behave systematically across INT4/INT6/INT8?\n\nBy holding rotation configuration and activation precision fixed, the\nRTN-vs-GPTQ comparison isolates the weight-quantization choice,\nwhich is exactly the contrast the question asks about.\n\n=========================================================================\n2. VERIFIED REPOSITORY & ENVIRONMENT STATE (OBSERVED FACTS ONLY)\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom direct inspection of the sandbox:\n  - /workspace contains exactly one file: task.json. There is NO QuaRot\n    codebase, README, config, script, or dataset artifact present.\n  - Searches for \"94328\", \"*qua*\", \".git\" directories, and Python files\n    outside system libs found nothing relevant. No model/dataset caches.\n  - Environment: Python 3.14.4; NO torch/transformers/datasets/\n    accelerate/lm-eval-harness/GPTQ packages installed.\n  - No GPU (gpu_enabled=false; 224 CPU cores); network unreachable\n    (/dev/tcp to 8.8.8.8 failed: \"Network is unreachable\").\nConsequence (already accepted by the researcher): the design below is\nwritten for a provisioning environment; all specifics of the real\nspcl/QuaRot repo are ASSUMPTIONS (Section 8) to be verified against the\nofficial repository when it is available, not observed facts.\n\n=========================================================================\n3. FIXED REQUIREMENTS vs PROPOSED CHOICES\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFIXED BY THE BRIEF (not negotiable):\n  F1  Model: Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct\n  F2  Precisions: INT4, INT6, INT8\n  F3  Methods: QuaRot-RTN, QuaRot-GPTQ\n  F4  Metrics: perplexity and zero-shot accuracy\n\nPROPOSED BY THIS DESIGN (researcher-adjustable, stated explicitly):\n  P1  6+2 zero-shot tasks: ARC-easy, ARC-challenge, HellaSwag, PIQA,\n      Winogrande, BoolQ (task accuracy + macro-average).\n  P2  PPL corpus: WikiText-2 test (sliding-window stride, seqlen 2048).\n  P3  Calibration: fixed 128-sample/2048-token subset, seeded document\n      selection, percdamp 0.01, groupsize 128; corpus matched to the\n      target repo's datautils loader (C4 'en' default, else WikiText-2\n      train split) — and NEVER the same split used for PPL evaluation.\n  P4  Replication: >=3 seeds per target cell (GPTQ is order/seed\n      dependent; RTN is deterministic but run under matched seed parity).\n  P5  Primary comparison is WEIGHT-ONLY (activations full precision):\n      this isolates the weight quantizer. W8A8 (QuaRot activation\n      quantization) is a clearly SECONDARY, optional robustness layer,\n      not part of the headline claim.\n  P6  Group size 128, true-sequential (repo default) quantization order.\n\n=========================================================================\n4. EXPERIMENTAL CONDITIONS AND CONTROLS\n=========================================================================\n4.1 Conditions table\n  ID   | Type                       | Method            | Bits | Notes\n  -----|----------------------------|-------------------|------|-------\n  R0   | Reference (control)        | None (FP16)      | 16   | upper bound\n  C4-R | Target cell (fixed req.)   | QuaRot-RTN        | 4    | rot+RTN\n  C6-R | Target cell (fixed req.)   | QuaRot-RTN        | 6    | rot+RTN\n  C8-R | Target cell (fixed req.)   | QuaRot-RTN        | 8    | rot+RTN\n  C4-G | Target cell (fixed req.)   | QuaRot-GPTQ       | 4    | rot+GPTQ\n  C6-G | Target cell (fixed req.)   | QuaRot-GPTQ       | 6    | rot+GPTQ\n  C8-G | Target cell (fixed req.)   | QuaRot-GPTQ       | 8    | rot+GPTQ\n  P4-R | Rotation-isolation control | Plain-RTN   (no rot) | 4 | proposed\n  P8-R | Rotation-isolation control | Plain-RTN   (no rot) | 8 | proposed\n  P4-G | Rotation-isolation control | Plain-GPTQ  (no rot) | 4 | proposed\n  P8-G | Rotation-isolation control | Plain-GPTQ  (no rot) | 8 | proposed\n\n  6 target cells (F2,F3) + FP16 reference (P-baseline) + 4 plain-quantizer\n  controls (P-controls). Plain controls at 6-bit are redundant for the\n  isolation question (rotation benefit is demonstrated at 4-bit and 8-bit),\n  so they are omitted to save compute; this is a proposed scope decision.\n\n4.2 Controls logic\n  - FP16 reference (R0): every metric is expressed relative to R0, which\n    neutralizes instruct-checkpoint quirks (instruct PPL runs slightly\n    higher than base-ckpt PPL; all conditions share this constant).\n  - Rotation-isolation controls (P4/8-*): identical quantizer configs with\n    --rotate OFF. The delta (QuaRot - plain) at the same bitwidth isolates\n    the value-add of Hadamard rotation; this is a sanity check that the\n    4-bit headline numbers are not driven purely by the quantizer.\n  - Protocol controls: identical calibration documents/order, identical\n    seeds, identical eval scripts, identical lm-eval harness version,\n    identical seqlen/stride, same GPU + torch settings across ALL rows.\n\n4.3 Activation quantization scope (secondary layer)\n  - Headline claim is derived from the weight-only table above (P5).\n  - Optionally, repeat the 6 target cells + R0 with QuaRot's 8-bit\n    activation quantization (W4A8/W6A8/W8A8), reported in a separate\n    appendix table and NEVER merged into the headline RTN-vs-GPTQ claim.\n\n=========================================================================\n5. CALIBRATION PROTOCOL (GPTQ path; RTN for parity)\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  - Corpus: MUST match the target repo's datautils loader. Default\n    expectation: C4 'en' (the GPTQ/QuaRot default); fallback: WikiText-2\n    train split. Confirm which the repo uses (ASSUMPTION A3).\n  - Size: fixed subset of 128 sequences, truncated/padded to seqlen 2048\n    tokens (repo --nsamples 128, --seqlen 2048 conventions).\n  - Seeded document selection: shuffle the corpus index with torch.Generator\n    seeded by the replication seed BEFORE slicing the 128 docs, so each\n    GPTQ seed quantizes a different-but-reproducible doc subset (order is\n    what drives GPTQ variance).\n  - Quantizer hyperparameters (fixed): --wbits 4|6|8, --groupsize 128,\n    --percdamp 0.01, --true-sequential on (repo default), rotation ON for\n    target cells, OFF for plain controls.\n  - RTN parity: RTN is data-independent, but each R-cell is launched under\n    the SAME process/seed initialization as its G-cell counterpart so that\n    RNG state differences cannot confound paired comparisons.\n  - Split hygiene: calibration split (C4 'en' or WikiText-2 TRAIN) must be\n    disjoint from the PPL evaluation split (WikiText-2 TEST). If the repo\n    defaults calibration to Wikitext-2, calibration must use the train\n    split only; the test split is reserved strictly for evaluation.\n  - Reproducibility record: log exact calibration corpus, doc indices,\n    seed, percdamp, groupsize, seqlen, and the repo commit hash.\n\n=========================================================================\n6. EVALUATION PROTOCOL\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------\n6.1 Perplexity\n  - Corpus: WikiText-2 TEST split (P2).\n  - Method: standard GPTQ/HF sliding-window evaluation, seqlen 2048,\n    stride such that documents are evaluated with overlapping windows\n    (e.g., stride = seqlen - 256; the exact repo convention is used);\n    report token-normalized model loss converted to PPL = exp(loss).\n  - Output: single mean PPL per (condition, seed).\n\n6.2 Zero-shot accuracy\n  - Harness: lm-eval-harness, PINNED version (proposed 0.4.x line; the\n    pinned version must match whatever the repo declares — ASSUMPTION A4).\n  - Tasks (P1): ARC-easy, ARC-challenge, HellaSwag, PIQA, Winogrande,\n    BoolQ. Report each task's standard accuracy metric (and acc_norm where\n    the harness defines it: HellaSwag, Winogrande, ARC).\n  - Instruct-model handling: NO chat template; plain causal-LM\n    continuation via HF AutoTokenizer, as in QuaRot's own eval.\n  - Output: per-task accuracy per (condition, seed) + macro-average across\n    the 6 tasks.\n\n6.3 Shared harness/eval settings across ALL conditions (including FP16)\n  - Same harness version, same task configs, same batch/tokenizer,\n    same seqlen; only the checkpoint differs. This makes all comparisons\n    relative-safe even if absolute numbers differ from published ones.\n\n=========================================================================\n7. ANALYSIS AND AGGREGATION PLAN\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------\n7.1 Per-cell summary\n  - For each cell: mean and SD over >=3 seeds of (a) WikiText-2 PPL,\n    (b) per-task accuracy, (c) task macro-average.\n7.2 Primary comparisons (weight-only table)\n  - Delta PPL:  dPPL(cell) = PPL(cell) - PPL(FP16);\n    relative increase rPPL(cell) = PPL(cell)/PPL(FP16) - 1. Lower is better.\n  - Accuracy: report per-task accuracy and macro-average; higher is better.\n7.3 Paired RTN vs GPTQ (matched seeds)\n  - For each bit b in {4,6,8}: paired difference (QuaRot-RTN - QuaRot-GPTQ)\n    over the same seeds for PPL (lower RTN = better) and for accuracy.\n    Report mean paired delta and, with n>=3 seeds, a paired t-test or\n    Wilcoxon signed-rank as an EXPLORATORY statistic (small n; interpret\n    cautiously, treat as descriptive evidence, not a definitive NHST).\n7.4 Monotonicity check across bits\n  - Verify, within each method, that rPPL decreases and macro-accuracy\n    increases (or is flat) as bits go 4 -> 6 -> 8, converging to the FP16\n    reference. The 6-bit point is the informative intermediate: it tests\n    whether 6-bit closes most of the 4-bit gap and whether RTN at 6-bit\n    approaches GPTQ at 6-bit.\n7.5 Rotation sanity check\n  - Compare QuaRot-* vs plain-* at 4-bit and 8-bit for both PPL and\n    accuracy. Expectation to be tested (not assumed as result): rotation\n    improves 4-bit PPL/accuracy substantially, more for RTN than GPTQ\n    (GPTQ already handles outliers better), and the benefit shrinks at\n    8-bit. This validates that the experimental setup reproduces QuaRot's\n    known mechanism on Phi-3.\n7.6 Reported artifacts\n  - Master table (columns: cell, PPL, dPPL, rPPL, macro-acc, per-task acc,\n    seeds); appendix W8A8 table; seed-level rows archived for audit.\n  - All numbers are DESIGNED OUTPUTS; nothing was run in this sandbox.\n\n=========================================================================\n8. REPO-SPECIFIC ASSUMPTIONS TO VERIFY AGAINST THE OFFICIAL REPO\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  A1  Phi-3 support / adaptation: The official spcl/QuaRot pipeline is\n      LLaMA-centric. Expected requirement: register Phi3ForCausalLM in the\n      supported model list and verify that Hadamard rotation is applied to\n      the correct weight/activation subspaces for Phi-3's rotary-embedding\n      layout (Phi-3 rotary embedding and head/QKV structure differ from\n      LLaMA). This is the top technical risk; the design budgets explicit\n      verification steps (patch, then check that quantization runs and\n      reconstruction loss is in a sane range) before any metric is trusted.\n      VERIFY, not assumed as working.\n  A2  6-bit (wbits=6) clean code path: README documents 4/8; assume the\n      quantizer accepts arbitrary wbits (standard GPTQ code does) but\n      verify there are no 4/8-only guards in packing/kernel paths, and that\n      W8A8 secondary runs at 6-bit weights. VERIFY.\n  A3  Calibration defaults: default corpus (expect C4 'en', 128 samples,\n      seqlen 2048) and datautils loader; the chosen calibration split must\n      not be the PPL evaluation split. VERIFY. \n  A4  Pinned lm-eval-harness version and Python compatibility. VERIFY.\n  A5  Script CLI surface (e.g., --rotate/--wbits/--groupsize/--percdamp/\n      --nsamples/--seed flags) exists as assumed in Sections 5-7. VERIFY.\n  A6  Model tokenizer/arch config available offline. VERIFY.\n\n=========================================================================\n9. FEASIBILITY AND RUNTIME REQUIREMENTS (PROVISIONED ENVIRONMENT)\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRequired runtime (the design targets this; it does NOT run in this sandbox):\n  - GPU: >=24GB VRAM (Phi-3-mini fp16 weights ~7.6GB + calibration\n    activations + GPTQ workspace; 24GB gives comfortable margin; 16GB\n    usable with CPU-offload but slower — flagged as a risk).\n  - Python <=3.11 (PyTorch/CUDA-stable line; sandbox Python 3.14.4 is\n    intentionally NOT suitable and is replaced by a provisioned venv).\n  - Official spcl/QuaRot dependency set: torch+CUDA, transformers,\n    datasets, lm-eval-harness (pinned), tqdm, and the repo's own deps.\n  - Network OR pre-cached HF assets: microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct\n    checkpoint, WikiText-2, C4 'en' calibration subset, lm-eval task data\n    (ARC/HellaSwag/PIQA/Winogrande/BoolQ). All cached offline beforehand.\n  - Disk: ~20GB for checkpoints/data; working disk for quantized artifacts.\nCompute estimate (rough, uncertain): GPTQ calibration on a 3.8B model is\nminutes per (seed, cell) on one GPU; lm-eval on 6 tasks ~30-60 min per\ncheckpoint. Full schedule (6 target cells x 3 seeds + FP16 + 4 plain\ncontrols + optional W8A8 appendix) completes within a day on one GPU.\nThis estimate is not a measured budget and carries uncertainty (Section 10).\n\n=========================================================================\n10. RISK REGISTER AND MITIGATIONS\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  Risk R1 (HIGH): Phi-3 not natively supported (A1). Mitigation: verify at\n      setup; budget adaptation patch + reconstruction-loss sanity check;\n      if the rotation placement cannot be validated for Phi-3, report this\n      as a blocking finding rather than fabricate numbers. NOTE: no such\n      verification was possible in this sandbox (repo absent).\n  Risk R2 (MED): 6-bit path (A2). Mitigation: early smoke run of C6-R/C6-G\n      before the full schedule.\n  Risk R3 (MED): calibration/eval split confusion (A3). Mitigation: strict\n      split rule (Section 5), logged in run record.\n  Risk R4 (MED): harness version drift (A4). Mitigation: pin version + repo\n      commit; all comparisons are within-version.\n  Risk R5 (LOW/MED): CPU-only fallback. Mitigation: design requires GPU;\n      cpu_affinity (224 cores) noted as a possible slow fallback only.\n  Risk R6 (LOW): GPTQ nondeterminism. Mitigation: >=3 seeds + fixed calib\n      doc order + recorded RNG state.\n\n=========================================================================\n11. SUBMISSION DELIVERABLES (what the final report will contain)\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  (1) Master weight-only results table (Section 7.6) with FP16 reference;\n  (2) rPPL and dPPL columns + per-task accuracy + macro-average;\n  (3) Seed-level archive (mean +/- SD);\n  (4) Paired RTN-vs-GPTQ deltas at 4/6/8 with sanity caveat;\n  (5) Monotonicity summary across bits;\n  (6) Rotation-sanity comparison (QuaRot vs plain);\n  (7) Optional W8A8 appendix clearly marked secondary;\n  (8) Environment/repo verification log including which assumptions\n      (A1-A6) were confirmed or found blocking.\n  All results would be produced by running the design; NONE were produced\n  or claimed here.\n=========================================================================\n\nEND OF DESIGN DOCUMENT\n=========================================================================", "evidence": "Verified sandbox state (all from direct inspection): `ls -la /workspace` = only task.json; no QuaRot repo/README/configs/scripts anywhere (searches for '94328', '*qua*', '.git'); `pip list` shows no torch/transformers/datasets/lm-eval-harness/gptq packages; Python 3.14.4; no GPU (gpu_enabled=false), 224 CPUs; network unreachable (TCP connect to 8.8.8.8:53 failed); no datasets/HF cache under /home/actor or /. task.json track.md: 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.' The design therefore specifies a provisioned runtime and marks every repo detail (A1-A6) as to-be-verified, not observed. No experiment was executed; all numbers in Section 11 are specified outputs only.", "assumptions": ["A1: official spcl/QuaRot is LLaMA-centric and needs Phi-3 registration + verification that Hadamard rotation is applied to the correct Phi-3 rotary/head/weight subspaces — the top risk, to verify, not assume working.", "A2: wbits=6 is a clean path in the repo quantizer (README documents 4/8; standard GPTQ accepts arbitrary bits); no 4/8-only guards.", "A3: repo calibration default ~ C4 'en', 128 samples, seqlen 2048 (else WikiText-2 train); calibration split is disjoint from WikiText-2 test used for PPL.", "A4/A5: pinned lm-eval-harness version compatible with the repo and Python <=3.11; CLI flags --rotate/--wbits/--groupsize/--percdamp/--nsamples/--seed exist as assumed.", "A6: Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct tokenizer/config available offline (pre-cached).", "Proposed-scope assumptions: 6-task zero-shot set (P1), WikiText-2 test PPL (P2), >=3 seeds (P4), weight-only primary with W8A8 secondary (P5), groupsize 128/true-sequential (P6), plain-quantizer controls at 4 and 8 bits only.", "Feasibility: provisioned env with >=24GB GPU, Python <=3.11, spcl/QuaRot deps, network or pre-cached HF assets; this sandbox cannot run it."], "uncertainties": ["Exact effort of Phi-3 adaptation (A1) and whether it becomes a blocking finding — cannot be checked from the absent repo.", "Whether 6-bit quantization runs cleanly through packing/kernels and the W8A8 path (A2).", "Actual repo calibration corpus/nsamples default (A3) and the pinned harness version (A4).", "Rough per-cell wall-clock (GPTQ calibration + lm-eval on 6 tasks) is an estimate, not measured.", "Whether the researcher wants 6-bit plain-quantizer controls added beyond the 4/8-bit isolation controls proposed (kept out to save compute)."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8ada39769dfa4573a916f5294bd90e67", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-8ada39769dfa4573a916f5294bd90e67", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"stage\": \"result\", \"summary\": \"Deliver complete final QuaRot-vs-Phi3 quantization experiment design\", \"answer\": \"===========================================